A||||11||Songman||88 DIS question for Ogre...||||1||
Z||000000||Songman||01-31-2001||07:28 PM||||or anyone else for that matter...<P>I finally got around to working on my 88 coupe that died on Christmas day. I had replaced the entire ignition system just a couple of months before (plugs, wires, module, coils, crank sensor, and ECM).<P>I mentioned this briefly before but I'll give a quick refresher since it has taken me awhile to work on it...<P>I was running along at about 70mph doing just fine and then all of a sudden nothing! Pulled over and the engine just cranked and cranked but wouldn't even try to hit. Gas smell was strong after this so I figured ignition.<P>Ogre said something about I might have not changed the shield over from my old module so I figured this must have been it.<P>Well, today I went out and got my new module and put it all together and popped it in the car... Hit the key and .... nothing... Crank crank crank and no hit.<P>What next? Could both coils have gone out at once? Crank sensor? Is there a fuse somewhere? I really thought the module would fix it...<P>Any experience would be appreciated. I ended up doing all of the above changes before costing me over $300 when all I needed was the module. I really don't want another wild goose chase if I can help it..<P>Thanks guys.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||01-31-2001||07:33 PM||||did you check and see if the vavle rockers are moving? you may have coughed up a cam gear if they aren't. You should be able to see at least a couple with the oil cap off.<P>Check all the fuses. make sure the ECM power feed by the battery is plugged in. I imagine they are fine but check anyway.<P>you need to check all the wiring. something may have come unplugged. It could be the crank sensor or the new module is trash. It happens. unlikely 2 coils are dead.<P>you didn't but Bosch platinum plugs in did you.<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ironhedno3||01-31-2001||07:44 PM||||maybe crank pos sensor..be sure and check all the wiring to the control module||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||01-31-2001||09:20 PM||||No rocker movement! I guess that is not a good thing! heheh<P>So... how is the 88 4 banger set up? Timing chain, belt, gears? Maybe it is time to think about pushing the 3800sc conversion up a little bit! <P>Thanks guys...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||01-31-2001||10:06 PM||||GEARS!||||reg||1
Z||000005||David Gamache||01-31-2001||10:24 PM||||I just had the same problem a few months back and had to replace the timing gears on my 88.  The timing gears are just 2 gears. Kragen auto parts has a set with a lifetime warranty for about 30.00$ and autozone has a set for a little over 20.00$. These sets are the fiber gears and after comparing parts the gears are the same from 84-88, which is not the same case for the cams. The 87-88 cam is different than the 86 and earlier even though many auto parts stores list the cams as the same. Anyway, Autozone can also order the aluminum/steel gear set for about 30.00$. I stuck with the orginal nylon fiber type, thought it might be quieter. The cam does have to be removed from the engine to press on/off the cam gear. This can be done with the engine in the car and the process takes about a full day. The Michiagan fiero club had a whole step by step on the timing gear removal procedure. As for the gaskets on the timing cover etc... I found the sealer called "Right Stuff" by permatex (it comes in a can that looks like a can of cheese whiz) is a great sealer. I have never had a leak using this stuff on timing covers,oil pans, valve covers, and so on. If you find any wear on the cam and need to replace it there are not any aftermarket cams avaliable that i could locate. Some companies said they could custom grind a cam but it was as much if not more than the factory cam. The factory cam runs about 150.00$ if you can find a GM parts distributor other than the dealer, who may cost even more. The lifters are roller and run about 26.00$ea. Thats probably more than you care to know, but I hope it helps.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||01-31-2001||11:08 PM||||Thanks David.. I didn't know it was that easy a job. It will be helpful to just get the Duke running for a little while longer until I can really afford to do the 3800SC change like I want to. You gave me just what I needed to know...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||01-31-2001||11:16 PM||||When you figure the original lasted 12 years+ I wouldn't worry too much about which type gear goes in it.||||reg||1
